PROMPT 2 — ROUTELESS DATABASE AND DATA ARCHITECTURE
ROLE

Act as a senior database architect and backend engineer.

Continue developing the existing ROUTELESS project. Do not recreate Prompt 1.

OBJECTIVE

Build a consistent data architecture supporting normal transportation, student journeys, driver management, kiosks and emergency transportation.

DATA MODELS

Create structured schemas for:

Users
Students
Drivers
Driver verification
Buses
Bus capacities
Routes
Stops
Trips
Bus locations
Bus availability
Schools and colleges
Student transport requests
Examination schedules
Road segments
Crisis events
Emergency allocations
Kiosks
Reports
Synchronization records
DEMONSTRATION DATA

Create realistic fictional records containing:

At least 5 villages.
At least 10 bus stops.
At least 8 buses.
At least 5 drivers.
At least 3 educational institutions.
Multiple connecting routes.
Different bus capacities.
Available and unavailable buses.
Delayed buses.
Examination-priority students.
Road closures.
Multiple student transportation requests.
DATA RULES
Every record must have a stable ID.
Validate relationships between records.
Use timestamps consistently.
Store region-specific information in data/configuration files.
Clearly distinguish demo and live records.
Never invent live transportation information.
Protect sensitive driver documents and student information.
IMPLEMENTATION

Create a data-access layer so frontend components do not directly depend on database implementation.

Use local demonstration storage initially. Prepare an optional Firebase adapter for later integration.

ACCEPTANCE TESTS
All seeded records load correctly.
Relationships are valid.
Bus capacities are available.
Routes connect valid stops.
Student requests reference valid destinations.
Examination records contain valid deadlines.
Data can be reset to its original state.
EXECUTION

Implement the actual schemas, seed data, validation and data-access services.

Preserve Prompt 1 functionality and prepare the application for Prompt 3.
